## Proof

The statement is **true**. In fact, a metric space is connected if and only if for every $\varepsilon > 0$ and every pair of points $x, y$, there is a finite $\varepsilon$-chain joining $x$ to $y$. We prove the forward direction (which is what is asked).

Fix an arbitrary $\varepsilon > 0$ and a base point $x \in X$. Define

$$A_\varepsilon = \{\, y \in X : \text{there exists a finite sequence } x = p_0, p_1, \dots, p_n = y \text{ with } d(p_i, p_{i+1}) < \varepsilon \,\}.$$

We claim $A_\varepsilon$ is both open and closed in $X$.

**$A_\varepsilon$ is open.** Let $y \in A_\varepsilon$, so there is an $\varepsilon$-chain $x = p_0, \dots, p_n = y$. For any $z$ with $d(y, z) < \varepsilon$, the sequence $x = p_0, \dots, p_n = y, p_{n+1} = z$ is an $\varepsilon$-chain from $x$ to $z$, since $d(p_n, p_{n+1}) = d(y, z) < \varepsilon$. Thus the open ball $B(y, \varepsilon) \subseteq A_\varepsilon$, proving $A_\varepsilon$ is open.

**$A_\varepsilon$ is closed.** Let $y \in \overline{A_\varepsilon}$. Then every open ball around $y$ meets $A_\varepsilon$; in particular $B(y, \varepsilon) \cap A_\varepsilon \neq \emptyset$. Pick $a \in A_\varepsilon \cap B(y, \varepsilon)$. Since $a \in A_\varepsilon$, there is an $\varepsilon$-chain $x = p_0, \dots, p_n = a$. Since $d(a, y) < \varepsilon$, appending $y$ gives an $\varepsilon$-chain $x = p_0, \dots, p_n = a, p_{n+1} = y$ from $x$ to $y$. Hence $y \in A_\varepsilon$, so $A_\varepsilon = \overline{A_\varepsilon}$, i.e., $A_\varepsilon$ is closed.

**Conclusion.** $A_\varepsilon$ is nonempty (it contains $x$, via the trivial chain $x = p_0$), and is both open and closed in $X$. Since $X$ is connected, the only nonempty clopen subset is $X$ itself. Therefore $A_\varepsilon = X$.

Since $\varepsilon > 0$ and $x \in X$ were arbitrary, for every pair of points $x, y \in X$ and every $\varepsilon > 0$ there exists a finite sequence of points in $X$ connecting $x$ to $y$ with consecutive distances less than $\varepsilon$. $\blacksquare$

